      "text": "To determine the area of figure \\(ABCD\\), we first need to find the area of each sector of the circle and then sum these areas.\n\n1. **Area of one sector:**\n   Each sector is a 60-degree sector of a circle with a radius of 12. The area of a sector of a circle is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius. For our sector, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n\n2. **Total area of two sectors:**\n   Since there are two such sectors (one on each side of the figure), the total area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{48\\pi}\\).",
