      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) consisting of two sectors of a circle with radius 12, each subtending a \\(60^\\circ\\) angle at the center.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Area of a Single Sector:**\n   - The formula for the area \\(A\\) of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in degrees) is:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n     \\]\n   - Here, \\(r = 12\\) and \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the Area of One Sector:**\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{sector}} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{6}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{sector}} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{sector}} = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Area of Figure \\(ABCD\\):**\n   - Since there are two such sectors, the total area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n     \\[\n     A_{\\text{total}} = 2 \\times A_{\\text{sector}}\n     \\]\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{total}} = 2 \\times 24\\pi\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{total}} = 48\\pi\n   \\]\n\n### Final Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi}\n\\]",
