      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the area of the figure $ABCD$, we need to consider the area of the two sectors of the circle and subtract the area of the triangle $ABD$ from the combined area of the sectors.\n\nFirst, let's determine the area of one sector. The circle has a radius of 12, and each sector has a central angle of $60^\\circ$. The area of a sector of a circle is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the central angle in degrees and $r$ is the radius of the circle. For one sector:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince we have two such sectors, the combined area of the sectors is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of triangle $ABD$. The angle $\\angle ABD$ is a central angle of $60^\\circ$, and since $AB = BC = CD = AD$ (because the sectors are equal and the circle is symmetric), triangle $ABD$ is an equilateral triangle. The side length $AB$ can be found using the chord length formula for a circle:\n\\[\nAB = 2r \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) = 2 \\times 12 \\sin\\left(\\frac{60^\\circ}{2}\\right) = 24 \\sin(30^\\circ) = 24 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 12\n\\]\nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side length $s$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of an equilateral triangle} = \\frac{s^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n Substituting $s = 12$:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of triangle } ABD = \\frac{12^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{144 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the area of figure $ABCD$ is the combined area of the sectors minus the area of triangle $ABD$:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } ABCD = 48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of figure $ABCD$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi - 36\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
